efs. Each chef can chop, mix, and plate one dish per second. But all ingredients must come from one narrow pantry aisle. If the aisle can deliver only 20 ingredients per second, the chefs will spend most of their time waiting. The kitchen\u2019s speed is not limited by the chefs\u2019 hands; it is limited by how fast ingredients can be moved.\n\nA graphics card works the same way. Its compute units are the chefs: they can do billions of math operations every second. Its memory is the pantry: it stores textures, colors, geometry, and other data. The memory bandwidth is the aisle: it is how many bytes per second can be moved between memory and the compute units. If a game needs lots of data\u2014like a high-resolution texture or many pixels\u2014the GPU may be able to calculate faster than the memory can feed it. Then the GPU is \u201cmemory-bound.\u201d\n\nHere is a simple made-up example. Suppose a GPU can do 100 trillion operations per second. Its memory can move 500 gigabytes per second. Imagine a workload where each", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0007314376416616142], [760, -7.908448219299316]], [[1144, -2.5749537599040195e-05], [1902, -11.155125617980957]], [[310, -0.623865008354187], [4087, -0.816682755947113]], [[5707, -0.7457325458526611], [4087, -0.7685825228691101]], [[310, -0.0680764839053154], [303, -2.7735323905944824]], [[1156, -2.1815538275404833e-05], [6163, -11.075587272644043]], [[25, -0.015275386162102222], [303, -4.25623083114624]], [[328, -0.24118703603744507], [10033, -2.0820112228393555]], [[814, -8.702656487002969e-05], [2523, -9.724316596984863]], [[20139, -1.2636264727916569e-05], [10083, -11.834782600402832]], [[310, -0.00017048382142093033], [2423, -8.863747596740723]], [[264, -0.00013149649021215737], [21520, -9.18400764465332]], [[21520, -0.0002222904295194894], [2754, -9.763598442077637]], [[1496, -1.4186006410454866e-05], [4962, -12.805184364318848]], [[33541, -0.00019796661217696965], [54087, -9.441703796386719]], [[5286, -3.9816695789340883e-